---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { links } from '../lib/site';
import vercel from '../../vercel.json';
import pkg from '../../package.json';

// The policy shown on this page is read from vercel.json at build time, so
// the page cannot drift from what the site actually sends.
const sent = vercel.headers.flatMap((rule) => rule.headers);
const csp = sent.find((h) => h.key === 'Content-Security-Policy')?.value ?? '';

const directiveNotes: Record<string, string> = {
  'default-src': 'Nothing loads unless one of the rules below allows it.',
  'script-src': 'Scripts come from this origin only. Inline scripts, eval and third-party scripts are all blocked.',
  'style-src': 'Stylesheets come from this origin only. Inline styles are blocked too.',
  'img-src': 'Images are served from this origin.',
  'font-src': 'Fonts are self-hosted, so no font CDN sees your visit.',
  'connect-src': 'Scripts can only make requests back to this origin.',
  'manifest-src': 'A web app manifest could only come from this origin.',
  'base-uri': 'No <base> tag can repoint relative URLs.',
  'form-action': 'No form on this site can submit anywhere.',
  'frame-ancestors': 'No other site can put this one in a frame, which rules out clickjacking.',
  'require-trusted-types-for':
    'In browsers with Trusted Types, assigning a string to innerHTML and similar sinks throws an error.',
  'trusted-types': 'No Trusted Types policy can be created to get around the rule above.',
  'upgrade-insecure-requests': 'Any http:// request is upgraded to https://.',
};

const directives = csp
  .split(';')
  .map((part) => part.trim())
  .filter(Boolean)
  .map((part) => {
    const [name = '', ...values] = part.split(/\s+/);
    return { name, value: values.join(' '), note: directiveNotes[name] ?? '' };
  });

const headerNotes: Record<string, string> = {
  'Strict-Transport-Security': 'Browsers that have visited once will only use HTTPS for the next two years.',
  'X-Content-Type-Options': 'Browsers must not guess a file type that differs from the one declared.',
  'X-Frame-Options': 'The older way to forbid framing, for browsers that ignore frame-ancestors.',
  'Referrer-Policy': 'Other sites you follow a link to see only this site’s origin, not the page you were on.',
  'Permissions-Policy': 'Camera, microphone, location and similar features are switched off for this site.',
  'Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy': 'A window opened from another site cannot keep a reference to this one.',
};

const otherHeaders = sent
  .filter((h) => h.key in headerNotes)
  .map((h) => ({ ...h, note: headerNotes[h.key] ?? '' }));

const runtime = Object.entries(pkg.dependencies);
const development = Object.entries(pkg.devDependencies);
const email = links.contact.find((item) => item.id === 'email');
const origin = Astro.site?.origin ?? '';
---

<Base
  title="Security"
  description="How this site is hardened: the Content-Security-Policy and other response headers it sends, how the terminal handles input, and what it depends on."
>
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>How this site is hardened</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A portfolio is a small target, but it is the one piece of my work anyone can inspect. This
        page lists what the site sends and why, so you can check it rather than take my word for it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose security">
      <h2 id="surface">What there is to attack</h2>
      <p>
        Not much, on purpose. The site is static HTML, CSS and a few kilobytes of JavaScript. There
        is no server code, database, login, cookie or form. Nothing you type is sent anywhere.
      </p>
      <p>That leaves a short list of realistic problems, and the rest of this page covers each one:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>script injected through a dependency or through content I write,</li>
        <li>the site being framed or tampered with in transit,</li>
        <li>a compromised package running at build time.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="csp">Content Security Policy</h2>
      <p>
        Every response carries this policy. It starts from <code>default-src 'none'</code> and allows
        only what the site uses. There is no <code>'unsafe-inline'</code> and no
        <code>'unsafe-eval'</code>.
      </p>
      <dl class="spec">
        {
          directives.map((d) => (
            <div>
              <dt>
                <code>
                  {d.name}
                  {d.value && ` ${d.value}`}
                </code>
              </dt>
              <dd>{d.note}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      <p>
        To make that possible the site has no inline scripts, inline styles or event-handler
        attributes at all. The build runs a check over the generated HTML and fails if one appears,
        or if any page references a script, stylesheet, image or font from another origin.
      </p>

      <h2 id="headers">Other response headers</h2>
      <dl class="spec">
        {
          otherHeaders.map((h) => (
            <div>
              <dt>
                <code>
                  {h.key}: {h.value}
                </code>
              </dt>
              <dd>{h.note}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <h2 id="terminal">The terminal</h2>
      <p>
        The terminal on the home page takes typed input, so it is the obvious place to try
        something. Whatever you type is written back to the page as text, never as HTML. The output
        of each command is rendered into the page when the site is built, and the script only copies
        those prepared blocks into view. Try <code>echo &lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;</code> and
        see.
      </p>
      <p>
        The script never calls <code>innerHTML</code>, <code>eval</code> or anything like them. In
        browsers that support Trusted Types, the policy above turns any such call into an error, so
        a mistake in a later change would fail loudly instead of becoming an injection bug.
      </p>

      <h2 id="dependencies">Dependencies</h2>
      <p>
        The site ships no JavaScript framework and loads nothing from third parties: no analytics,
        no font CDN, no embeds. Fonts are served from this origin. Build dependencies are pinned to
        exact versions and installed from a lockfile.
      </p>
      <dl class="spec spec--compact">
        {
          runtime.map(([name, version]) => (
            <div>
              <dt>
                <code>{name}</code>
              </dt>
              <dd>{version} (build)</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
        {
          development.map(([name, version]) => (
            <div>
              <dt>
                <code>{name}</code>
              </dt>
              <dd>{version} (type checking only)</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <h2 id="limits">What this does not cover</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The headers are set in the hosting configuration, so the build cannot prove they are being
          sent. Check the live response yourself with the command below.
        </li>
        <li>
          A strict policy limits what injected script could do. It does not replace reviewing what
          goes into the build.
        </li>
        <li>I rely on the host for TLS, availability and keeping its own platform patched.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="verify">Check it yourself</h2>
      <pre><code>curl -sI {origin}/</code></pre>
      <p>
        If what comes back does not match this page, or you find something I missed, I would like to
        hear about it{email && <Fragment>: <a href={email.href}>{email.text}</a></Fragment>}. Contact
        details for reports are also in <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">security.txt</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="security__aside">
        There are also three flags hidden around this site, in the form <code>FLAG&#123;...&#125;</code>.
        If you find one, <code>submit</code> it in the terminal on the home page. They are checked in
        your browser, so they are a game and not a security control.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .security {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-6);
  }

  .spec {
    border-block-start: var(--hairline) solid var(--border);
  }

  .spec > div {
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    border-block-end: var(--hairline) solid var(--border);
  }

  .spec dt code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .spec dd {
    margin-block-start: 0.375rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .spec--compact > div {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.25rem var(--s-2);
  }

  .spec--compact dd {
    margin-block-start: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .security__aside {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-6);
    padding-block-start: var(--s-3);
    border-block-start: var(--hairline) solid var(--border);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
  }
</style>
